# Hostile audit — uniform pentagon sea beyond the old `1.8623` endpoint

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Reviewed:** `research/target_c_uniform_pentagon_sea_extended_range_2026-09-15.md`  
**Disposition:** **PASS at its stated full-core train-measure scope.**  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The candidate claims that the modern pentagon-sea algebra was artificially truncated at `t<=1.8623`: the matched all-train theorem used by that algebra is uniform across the full-core Type-II train-measure branch.  This audit checks the theorem scope, the face identities, the asymptotic directions, and the widened Branch-R use.

## 1. The `g=0.007707` all-train theorem is genuinely uniform in `t`

The hostile audit of the modern chamber-surplus theorem explicitly checked this exact issue.  It records the scope chain

```text
g=0.007707 artifact
 -> same scope as hostile-audited g=0.0077
 -> same scope as hostile-audited g=0.0076
 -> canonical full-core Type-II train-measure branch.
```

The audit further states that the proof is a uniform three-branch phase-star capacity argument and is **not** conditional on `t<=1.844`, residual localization, or any later high-`t` path theorem.

Therefore

\[
\liminf {T_{train}-q/2\over D}
\ge C_{707}
\]

is available at every fixed `t` endpoint in the full-core train-measure branch.

**Verdict:** PASS.  Extending the algebra past `1.8623` introduces no scope mismatch.

## 2. The face floor has the correct sign in every substitution

The exact stopped-train identity is

\[
T_{train}-q=2f-k.
\]

With `t=(q-k)/D` and `kappa=k/D>=0`,

\[
{T_{train}-q/2\over D}
={t\over2}+2{f\over D}-{\kappa\over2}.
\]

Thus at an upper endpoint `t<=X`, dropping `kappa/2` and replacing `t` by the larger `X` weakens the resulting lower bound:

\[
f/D\ge C_{707}/2-X/4-o(1).
\]

There is no reversal here.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 3. The `Q` upper envelope is independent of the path program

Canonical loaded-hull bookkeeping gives

\[
b+Q=2(q-k).
\]

The occurrence-counted outer contact walk contains the force-hull boundary, so

\[
b\ge\operatorname{per}(F).
\]

Full-core Type II has

\[
\operatorname{per}(F)/D\to\pi.
\]

Hence for `t<=X`,

\[
Q/D\le2X-\pi+o(1).
\]

No O--O terminal-energy or path hypothesis is involved.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 4. The pentagon floor is exact degree arithmetic

For bounded exceptional face counts `f_d`,

\[
f=\sum_{d\ge5}f_d,
\qquad
Q=\sum_{d\ge5}(d-4)f_d.
\]

Then

\[
Q-f=\sum_{d\ge6}(d-5)f_d\ge\sum_{d\ge6}f_d=f-P_5,
\]

so

\[
P_5\ge2f-Q.
\]

Inserting the adverse endpoint envelopes gives

\[
P_5/D\ge C_{707}+\pi-{5X\over2}-o(1).
\]

The right side is positive up to

\[
X<{2(C_{707}+\pi)\over5}.
\]

The exact certificate gives a lower enclosure of this threshold above `2.0215`, so the displayed `2.020` endpoint is conservative.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 5. The train-touching count remains valid at the wider endpoint

Every pentagon contributes five terminal face-side occurrences to the complete stopped-train decomposition.  Every complete stopped train has at most two terminal occurrences.  Therefore

\[
N_5\ge{5P_5\over2}
\]

without any phase assumption.  The merged isolate-host theorem separately proves every such pentagon has empty source interior.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 6. Branch R also has uniform scope

The modern train-slack floor

\[
\liminf \tau_D/D\ge0.7467921
\]

comes from the uniform all-train gap plus full-core asymptotics; its hostile audit does not impose `t<=1.8623`.

The exact decomposition

\[
2\tau_D=\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R
\]

gives the global full-core split

```text
rho_rec >= tau0 D  -> singleton branch;
rho_rec <  tau0 D  -> sigma_R > tau0 D.
```

In the second branch the rhombus/pentagon incidence inequality

\[
I_5\ge5(2f-Q)-\tau_0D-o(D)
\]

uses only global face/recipient bookkeeping.  Substituting the same adverse `f,Q` endpoint envelopes is therefore valid beyond `1.8623`.

Its scalar lower envelope stays positive to

\[
X<{2(C_{707}+\pi-\tau_0/5)\over5},
\]

whose exact lower enclosure is above `1.9618`.

**Verdict:** PASS.

